## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates non-branching, low-level bit-twiddling mastery and tests the ability to work with 128-bit data using traditional C structures.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount algorithm
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves
- Non-branching reduction using masks and shifts

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    x = (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL);
    return (unsigned int)x;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes low-level bit-tracking across a 128-bit value, edge cases at zero, and portable handling when single-word intrinsics are unavailable. It mirrors real-world tasks where bitmaps or flags span multiple words.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit split into two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count logic with early non-zero word optimizations
- Portable fallback for environments lacking 64-bit ctzn intrinsics
- Edge case for zero input

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  if (x.lo) return __builtin_ctzll(x.lo);
  if (x.hi) return 64 + __builtin_ctzll(x.hi);
  return 128;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to implement without __builtin_ctzll?
- How would you adapt for architectures without 64-bit support?
- Extend to 256-bit using a similar two-word strategy and discuss performance implications.

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates practical bitfield packing/unpacking and rotation without branches, mirroring real-world protocol parsing.

## Key Concepts
- Field extraction with masks and shifts
- Recombination to rotate fields in place
- Boundary safety for 10-bit widths

## Code Example
```javascript
function rotateFieldsLeft32(x) {
  const A = x & 0x3FF;
  const B = (x >>> 10) & 0x3FF;
  const C = (x >>> 20) & 0x3FF;
  return (B) | (C << 10) | (A << 20);
}
```

